---INSTRUCTION---
Write an Elixir function that, given a list of lists, returns a list of tuples representing the Cartesian product of all the inner lists. The function should take one argument, a list of lists, and return a list of tuples. Each tuple in the output contains one element from each inner list, in the same order as the input lists. The Cartesian product of an empty list of lists is defined to be a list containing one empty tuple. If any inner list is empty, the result is an empty list because no combinations can be formed. The function should be named `possible_permutations` and placed in a module named `Solution`. Assume that the elements of the inner lists can be of any type. The function should not use any external libraries for the Cartesian product and should implement the logic itself. The output size grows exponentially with the number of inner lists, as it is the product of their lengths.

---TEST---
```elixir
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "list of two lists" do
    assert Solution.possible_permutations([[1, 2], [3, 4]]) == [{1, 3}, {1, 4}, {2, 3}, {2, 4}]
  end

  test "list of three single-element lists" do
    assert Solution.possible_permutations([[1], [2], [3]]) == [{1, 2, 3}]
  end

  test "list of three lists with two elements each" do
    assert Solution.possible_permutations([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]) == [
             {1, 3, 5},
             {1, 3, 6},
             {1, 4, 5},
             {1, 4, 6},
             {2, 3, 5},
             {2, 3, 6},
             {2, 4, 5},
             {2, 4, 6}
           ]
  end
end
```

---REFERENCE---
```elixir
defmodule Solution do
  @spec possible_permutations([list()]) :: [tuple()]
  def possible_permutations(list_of_lists) do
    Enum.reduce(list_of_lists, [{}], fn list, acc ->
      for tuple <- acc, element <- list do
        Tuple.append(tuple, element)
      end
    end)
  end
end
```
